WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:04.600
They made cutting class an art form&nbsp;
and gave an entire teen generation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:08.960
a new perspective on life, love, and&nbsp;
proper car care. So what's the cast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:14.160
of "Ferris Bueller's Day Off" doing now?&nbsp;
The question is, What aren’t they doing?

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:17.800
Though "Ferris Bueller's Day Off"&nbsp;
remains the defining film of his career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:22.000
Matthew Broderick's breakout role&nbsp;
actually arrived three years earlier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:26.920
when he played computer hacker David in&nbsp;
the cyber-drama "WarGames." A part in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:31.000
"Ladyhawke" soon followed, and he then&nbsp;
became everyone's favorite enterprising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:36.399
teen when John Hughes cast him in the title&nbsp;
role of 1986’s "Ferris Bueller's Day Off."

00:00:36.399 --> 00:00:45.040
"Life moves pretty fast. If you don't stop and&nbsp;
look around once in a while, you could miss it."

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:49.400
After "Ferris" was a hit, Broderick kept&nbsp;
working steadily in film and television.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:54.160
The decade following that film brought leading&nbsp;
roles in "Glory," "The Freshman," and "The Cable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:59.200
Guy," not to mention the voice of the adult&nbsp;
Simba in the iconic Disney classic "The Lion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:04.960
King." Other memorable movies for Broderick&nbsp;
include 1998's "Godzilla," "Election," and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:09.640
Disney's "Inspector Gadget." But Broderick&nbsp;
was also an accomplished stage actor and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:13.680
used that experience when he nabbed&nbsp;
the starring role in the 2001 stage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:18.960
adaptation of Mel Brooks' "The Producers"&nbsp;
and then its 2005 film. Twenty years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:24.240
he still divides his work between stage and&nbsp;
screen, and in 2023 he had a supporting role in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:29.840
the adult comedy "No Hard Feelings" as well as a&nbsp;
lead role in the Netflix miniseries "Painkiller."

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:36.320
Like his co-star, Alan Ruck's career also started&nbsp;
to take off in the early 1980s. After early roles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:41.560
in films like "Class" and "Bad Boys," he landed&nbsp;
the part of Ferris' best friend Cameron Frye&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:46.920
in "Ferris Bueller's Day Off." Ruck had the task&nbsp;
of playing everything Broderick's Ferris is not,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:52.588
and he did it so well that he steals just about&nbsp;
every scene in the movie with his anxious comedy.

00:01:52.588 --> 00:01:55.828
"Cameron what's gonna happen&nbsp;
to it, it's in a garage!"

00:01:55.954 --> 00:02:00.994
"It could get wrecked, stolen,&nbsp;
scratched - breathed on wrong!"

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:04.920
Ruck continued to act regularly&nbsp;
through the rest of the 1980s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:10.080
but the 1990s was where he really got prolific&nbsp;
with his work on the big and small screens. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:14.280
you only know him as Cameron, you might&nbsp;
be surprised to realize that he was also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:19.120
gunslinger Hendry French in "Young Guns II,"&nbsp;
and played Captain Harriman in "Star Trek:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:22.240
Generations." He landed on&nbsp;
television not long after,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:28.000
starring alongside Michael J. Fox in the network&nbsp;
hit "Spin City" as Chief of Staff Stuart Bondeck.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:31.520
More than a decade later, he joined&nbsp;
the main cast of the short-lived TV&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:36.960
remake of "The Exorcist" as Henry Rance, and&nbsp;
beginning in 2018 was part of the ensemble of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:41.320
HBO's award-winning drama "Succession."&nbsp;
He's never left the movies, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.320 --> 00:02:48.080
appearing in "War Machine" in 2017, 2023's&nbsp;
"The Burial," and the sequel to "Wind River."

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:52.880
Aside from "Ferris Bueller's Day Off," Mia Sara&nbsp;
is also remembered for her standout performance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:58.120
as Princess Lili in the Ridley Scott-directed&nbsp;
fantasy epic "Legend" opposite Tom Cruise. She&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:01.880
followed that up the very next year when&nbsp;
she took on the part of Ferris' aloof but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:06.600
charming girlfriend Sloane Peterson. As&nbsp;
Sloane, Sara added a level of cool to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:11.480
the often goofy antics of her co-stars while&nbsp;
still being in on the joke the entire time.

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:16.440
Of course, Hollywood took notice of this opening&nbsp;
one-two punch, and Sara spent the next two decades&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:20.840
working regularly in film and television.&nbsp;
Unfortunately, she struggled to find the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:25.760
level of success as she did in the mid-'80s.&nbsp;
While she did appear in some popular films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:30.640
including "Timecop" as the wife of Jean-Claude&nbsp;
Van Damme's character, her later career never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:35.280
matched her mid-'80s hits. That said, she&nbsp;
is notable for being the first actor to play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:41.000
Harley Quinn in live-action, nabbing the role in&nbsp;
the short-lived "Birds of Prey" series in 2002.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:45.400
Sara stepped away from acting in the early&nbsp;
2010s, and one of her final roles was as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:50.800
Princess Langwidere in the film "Dorothy and&nbsp;
the Witches of Oz" and its miniseries. After&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:55.960
leaving Hollywood, Sara embarked on a career&nbsp;
as a poet and devoted time to her family.

00:03:55.960 --> 00:04:00.880
Though Jennifer Grey only performed in fewer&nbsp;
than a dozen roles over the course of the '80s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:06.800
several of them proved to be classics. In 1984&nbsp;
she co-starred in "Red Dawn" with Patrick Swayze&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:10.760
and had a small role in "The Cotton Club"&nbsp;
from director Francis Ford Coppola. Then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:15.200
she played Jeanie, the smarmy, vindictive&nbsp;
sister of Matthew Broderick's Ferris in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:19.120
"Ferris Bueller's Day Off." While that&nbsp;
part garnered some attention for her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:23.320
it was her role as Baby in 1987's&nbsp;
"Dirty Dancing" that made her a star.

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:29.080
Off-screen, however, Grey was quietly dating her&nbsp;
"Bueller" co-star and on-screen brother Broderick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:33.520
and the pair were involved in a devastating&nbsp;
head-on car collision just before the release of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:38.400
"Dirty Dancing." A woman and her adult daughter&nbsp;
were both killed as a result of the crash,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:43.440
and the tragedy haunted Broderick and Grey&nbsp;
for many years, physically and emotionally.

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:48.320
Over the next decade, Grey continued to work but&nbsp;
failed to regain her star status, appearing in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:52.880
lesser-known films like "Wind" and "Red Meat."&nbsp;
She took a break from acting after the birth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:57.880
of her daughter in the early 2000s and appeared&nbsp;
mostly on TV after her return, which also included&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.880 --> 00:05:02.280
a memorable appearance on Season 11 of&nbsp;
"Dancing with the Stars." More recently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:08.040
Grey showed up in recurring roles on "Grey's&nbsp;
Anatomy" and the "Roseanne" reboot "The Conners."

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:12.760
Lyman Ward's screen acting career stretches&nbsp;
all the way back to the 1970s, when he began&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.760 --> 00:05:17.840
appearing in guest roles on TV show classics&nbsp;
like "Bonanza," "Battlestar Galactica," "One&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:22.320
Day at a Time," and the very first episode&nbsp;
of "Laverne &amp; Shirley." He continued to work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:27.200
regularly throughout that decade, and by the&nbsp;
1980s he was appearing in films like "Protocol,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:32.080
"Moscow on the Hudson," and "A Nightmare on&nbsp;
Elm Street 2: Freddy's Revenge." As Ferris&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:37.040
Bueller's father in "Day Off," he brings a&nbsp;
sense of likable cluelessness to the comedy

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:41.760
After "Ferris Bueller," Ward stayed working, with&nbsp;
credits including "Murder, She Wrote," "Family&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:47.640
Ties," "JAG," "Monk," and many more. In the 2010s&nbsp;
his acting work began to slow down, though he had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:52.680
a major role in an episode of "Transparent"&nbsp;
in 2015. Hollywood wasn't his only career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:58.240
though. Turning to writing in the 2010s, Ward&nbsp;
published his first novel, "Fortune's Tide."

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:02.920
Cindy Pickett's breakthrough as a screen&nbsp;
actor came in 1977 when she was cast on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.920 --> 00:06:08.280
the long-running soap opera "Guiding Light,"&nbsp;
where she appeared in 22 episodes. By the 1980s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:13.880
more TV and film roles were flowing in, including&nbsp;
appearances on some major hits like "Magnum, P.I."

00:06:13.880 --> 00:06:16.480
She secured the role of&nbsp;
Ferris Bueller's mother Katie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.480 --> 00:06:21.120
who plays opposite star Matthew Broderick in&nbsp;
some of the film's funniest early moments as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:25.200
the teen uses her to convince the&nbsp;
world that he's sick. Oddly enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:31.040
her on-screen husband Lyman Ward later became her&nbsp;
real-life husband, though they divorced in 1992.

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:35.840
After "Bueller," Pickett showed up&nbsp;
in the 1987 TV miniseries "Amerika"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:40.800
and the "Abyss" knock-off "DeepStar Six."&nbsp;
In the 2000s, she was as active as ever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:45.200
making single-episode stints in shows like&nbsp;
"Burn Notice," "Saving Grace," "The Client&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:49.680
List," and "The Mentalist." Outside of "Ferris&nbsp;
Bueller's Day Off," of course, Pickett is best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:55.480
known for her role as Dr. Carol Novino in the&nbsp;
award-winning medical drama "St. Elsewhere."

00:06:55.480 --> 00:07:00.480
Edie McClurg became beloved to audiences of&nbsp;
the '80s thanks in no small part to her role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:05.680
as Principal Rooney's eccentric secretary,&nbsp;
Grace, in "Ferris Bueller's Day Off."

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:10.280
"He jeopardizes my ability to&nbsp;
effectively govern this student body."

00:07:10.280 --> 00:07:13.760
"Well, makes you look like&nbsp;
an ass is what he does, Ed."

00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:18.040
She was no newcomer to Hollywood, though, and&nbsp;
in the mid-1970s she had been a regular on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.040 --> 00:07:22.920
"The Richard Pryor Show" and "Tony Orlando&nbsp;
and Dawn" and had a brief role in the film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.920 --> 00:07:28.120
"Carrie" before she hit her stride the following&nbsp;
decade on "Harper Valley PTA" and "The Dukes."

00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:32.960
At the movies, outside of her role as Grace in&nbsp;
"Bueller," McClurg was a recurring presence in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:38.040
Cheech &amp; Chong projects like "Cheech &amp; Chong's&nbsp;
Next Movie" and "The Corsican Brothers." She&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:43.400
also had cameos in "Mr. Mom" and "Planes, Trains&nbsp;
&amp; Automobiles" before her appearance as Chastity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.400 --> 00:07:48.840
Pariah in "Elvira: Mistress of the Dark." The&nbsp;
'90s saw her in a number of fan-favorite films&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:53.560
like "Flubber" and "Natural Born Killers,"&nbsp;
as well as a flurry of major network hits.

00:07:53.560 --> 00:07:57.280
McClurg's voice is one of the things that&nbsp;
made her distinctive, too, and she's lent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.280 --> 00:08:02.760
it to animation many times, from "The Rugrats&nbsp;
Movie" to "Wreck-It Ralph." Sadly, in 2019,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:07.920
McClurg's health began to decline, and her family&nbsp;
appointed a conservator to manage her affairs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:13.040
as she was no longer able to live unassisted.&nbsp;
In 2022, she was tragically the victim of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.040 --> 00:08:17.600
elder abuse and alleged sexual assault,&nbsp;
and her family successfully got an order&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:22.960
of protection against a longtime friend who&nbsp;
was alleged to have attempted to defraud her.

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:26.960
There are a few examples in "Ferris Bueller's&nbsp;
Day Off" of actors who come in for just one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:31.320
scene but leave a lasting impression as&nbsp;
they get roped into Ferris' shenanigans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.320 --> 00:08:36.560
and schemes. Jonathan Schmock is arguably the&nbsp;
most memorable of these actors as the maitre&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:41.840
d' at a restaurant who refuses to believe&nbsp;
Ferris is the "Sausage King of Chicago."

00:08:41.840 --> 00:08:44.520
"Are you suggesting I'm not who I say I am?"

00:08:44.520 --> 00:08:47.600
"I'm suggesting that you leave&nbsp;
before I have to get snooty."

00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:48.520
"Snooty?"

00:08:48.646 --> 00:08:50.646
"Snotty."
 
"Snotty?"

00:08:50.646 --> 00:08:54.880
Schmock had already been working for a few years&nbsp;
by the time he was hired for "Ferris Bueller,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.880 --> 00:08:59.840
most notably on the TV series "Double Trouble,"&nbsp;
and has continued to make regular appearances in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:05.760
film and TV. His more recent work includes guest&nbsp;
spots on "The Goldbergs," "Flaked," "Transparent,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:10.720
"2 Broke Girls," and more, but he's not just a&nbsp;
busy actor — Schmock is also an accomplished and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.720 --> 00:09:15.320
prolific screenwriter, particularly in the&nbsp;
realm of TV comedies. His credits include&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.320 --> 00:09:19.720
co-developing "Sabrina the Teenage Witch,"&nbsp;
co-creating "Brotherly Love," and scripting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.720 --> 00:09:25.080
for shows like "Blossom," "Dharma &amp; Greg," "Real&nbsp;
Time with Bill Maher," and "Young and Hungry."

00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:30.480
Charlie Sheen has made a career as Hollywood's bad&nbsp;
boy. That image is perfectly apropos, considering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:36.200
his part in "Ferris Bueller's Day Off" as a punk&nbsp;
teenager who romances Jennifer Grey's character.

00:09:36.200 --> 00:09:41.480
"I meant are you in here for drugs?"

00:09:41.581 --> 00:09:42.941
"Why are you here?"

00:09:42.941 --> 00:09:44.381
"Drugs."

00:09:44.381 --> 00:09:49.560
He took the role when he was on the cusp of&nbsp;
superstardom. The son of famed actor Martin Sheen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.560 --> 00:09:54.320
Charlie had already appeared alongside Grey in&nbsp;
"Red Dawn," and after "Bueller" the actor had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.320 --> 00:09:59.080
a spectacular run that includes classics like&nbsp;
"Wall Street," "Platoon," and "Major League."

00:09:59.080 --> 00:10:03.960
Sheen parlayed those '80s hits into modest&nbsp;
'90s success: After a pair of "Hot Shots"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.960 --> 00:10:08.280
parody movies, he tried his hand at becoming&nbsp;
an action star with "The Chase," "Terminal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.280 --> 00:10:12.840
Velocity," and the sci-fi thriller "The&nbsp;
Arrival." That never really panned out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.840 --> 00:10:18.201
but in 2000 he took over for Michael J.&nbsp;
Fox as the lead in "Spin City." In 2003,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.201 --> 00:10:22.360
Sheen was cast as Charlie Harper in the hit&nbsp;
sitcom "Two and a Half Men." He remained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.360 --> 00:10:26.960
on the show until 2011, when clashes&nbsp;
with Chuck Lorre led to his departure.

00:10:26.960 --> 00:10:32.000
A series of very public issues including drug&nbsp;
abuse and messy divorces plagued Sheen in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:36.840
the 2010s, but in 2019, he revealed that&nbsp;
he'd been sober for more than a year and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.840 --> 00:10:41.160
was in therapy. In 2023, he and Lorre&nbsp;
buried the hatchet, and the actor was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.160 --> 00:10:46.362
handed a recurring role in the producer's&nbsp;
upcoming Max comedy "How to Be a Bookie."

00:10:46.614 --> 00:10:54.040
"Bueller? Bueller? Bueller?"

00:10:55.480 --> 00:11:01.000
Actor Ben Stein appears in only two scenes,&nbsp;
but he so perfectly embodies the archetype of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:06.160
the tedious, droning high school teacher that&nbsp;
he carved out a niche as Hollywood's go-to guy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.160 --> 00:11:10.840
for playing monotone bores. Most memorably,&nbsp;
he portrayed teacher Mr. Cantwell in "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.840 --> 00:11:16.520
Wonder Years," an airport customer service rep in&nbsp;
"Planes, Trains &amp; Automobiles," and Rabbi Goldberg&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.520 --> 00:11:22.160
on "Family Guy," not counting numerous cameos&nbsp;
as himself in a long-running eyedrops campaign.

00:11:22.160 --> 00:11:26.760
Off-screen, Stein was a speechwriter for&nbsp;
Presidents Richard Nixon and Gerald Ford&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.760 --> 00:11:30.360
and has written extensively on economics and&nbsp;
legal issues for the Wall Street Journal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.360 --> 00:11:34.920
Fortune, and The New York Times. He starred&nbsp;
in two shows for Comedy Central: the talker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.920 --> 00:11:40.200
"Turn Ben Stein On" and the game show "Win Ben&nbsp;
Stein's Money," where contestants who demonstrated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.200 --> 00:11:44.360
more knowledge than the well-educated,&nbsp;
well-read Stein won a portion of his salary.

00:11:44.360 --> 00:11:48.520
In recent years, Stein has mostly stayed&nbsp;
away from Hollywood and focused more on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.520 --> 00:11:53.360
being a spokesperson for the Republican Party. He&nbsp;
frequently appears on outlets like Fox News and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.360 --> 00:12:00.600
Newsmax. In 2023 he came under fire for racist&nbsp;
comments made about Aunt Jemima pancake syrup.

00:12:00.600 --> 00:12:05.160
Kristy Swanson's role as Ferris' classmate&nbsp;
Simone Adamley wasn't quite a standout,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.160 --> 00:12:10.560
but audiences still know her, because less than&nbsp;
a decade on, Swanson originated the role of Buffy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.560 --> 00:12:15.760
in the "Buffy the Vampire Slayer" franchise. She&nbsp;
also played the titular role in "Mannequin Two:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.760 --> 00:12:20.080
On the Move," taking over for Kim&nbsp;
Cattrall from the 1987 cult classic.

00:12:20.080 --> 00:12:24.840
Swanson's career in the 1990s was hit-or-miss,&nbsp;
but she did have an important role in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.840 --> 00:12:29.240
well-received but ultimately doomed series&nbsp;
"Early Edition." She also appeared in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.240 --> 00:12:34.320
variety of popular films, including the critically&nbsp;
acclaimed John Singleton drama "Higher Learning"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.320 --> 00:12:39.480
and the Joe Pesci crime comedy "8 Heads in a&nbsp;
Duffel Bag." Into the 2000s it was mostly TV&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.480 --> 00:12:44.640
where Swanson made a name for herself, with a&nbsp;
recurring role in "Psych" and episodes of "CSI:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.640 --> 00:12:48.800
Miami" and "Just Shoot Me!" Though she's&nbsp;
mostly acted in cable channel TV movies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.800 --> 00:12:54.640
in the last decade, she did appear in a&nbsp;
trio of episodes of "SEAL Team" in 2019.

00:12:54.640 --> 00:12:58.280
Actor Richard Edson plays the garage&nbsp;
attendant who sneaks off with Cameron's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.280 --> 00:13:01.800
car and goes for a joyride. It&nbsp;
was just his fourth acting role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.800 --> 00:13:06.800
and he barely got any lines, but he appeared&nbsp;
in a host of major movies in the ensuing years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.800 --> 00:13:10.640
Beginning with "Howard the Duck" and&nbsp;
"Platoon" the same year, Edson went on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.640 --> 00:13:16.080
to show up as Pvt. Abersold in "Good Morning,&nbsp;
Vietnam" and as Vito in "Do the Right Thing."

00:13:16.080 --> 00:13:20.600
Edson's unique look allowed him to stand out,&nbsp;
too, and that has perhaps been the reason that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.600 --> 00:13:26.120
he's never been without work, even if he never did&nbsp;
become a big star. The '90s saw him showing up all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.120 --> 00:13:31.440
over film and TV, whether it was "The Adventures&nbsp;
of Pete &amp; Pete" or the much-maligned "Super Mario&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.440 --> 00:13:36.200
Bros." movie, in which he played Spike Koopa. We&nbsp;
could go on forever with all of the projects that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.200 --> 00:13:41.200
Edson has worked on, but no doubt you've seen him&nbsp;
around, as he's kept himself busy year after year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.200 --> 00:13:46.640
with one of his more recent projects&nbsp;
being the indie film "Seeing Red" in 2022.

00:13:46.640 --> 00:13:50.560
Not only was "Ferris Bueller" one of&nbsp;
Louie Anderson's first acting experiences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.560 --> 00:13:55.440
his comedy career was also just getting started.&nbsp;
His role in the film had him playing a flower&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.440 --> 00:14:00.440
deliveryman and gave him no lines at all. The&nbsp;
movie came to theaters just two years before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.440 --> 00:14:06.080
his first starring role, in 1988's "The Wrong&nbsp;
Guys," which was based on a story by John Hughes.

00:14:06.080 --> 00:14:11.600
By the early '90s, Anderson was a huge comedy&nbsp;
star, and in 1994 he got his own animated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.600 --> 00:14:16.560
kid's show, "Life with Louie," which ran for&nbsp;
three seasons, then "The Louie Show." Though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.560 --> 00:14:20.880
Anderson continued with standup and could be&nbsp;
seen regularly on television, he became best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.880 --> 00:14:26.200
known for game shows. In the early 2000s, he was&nbsp;
hired as the host of the "Family Feud" revival&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.200 --> 00:14:30.760
and made many appearances on "The New Hollywood&nbsp;
Squares" and "Hollywood Squares." He'd return&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.760 --> 00:14:35.400
to the circuit in 2017, frequently appearing&nbsp;
as a guest star on "Funny You Should Ask."

00:14:35.400 --> 00:14:40.320
In some of his final acting roles, Anderson&nbsp;
appeared on "Young Sheldon" in 2020 and had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.320 --> 00:14:45.080
roles in "Search Party" that same year&nbsp;
and "No Activity" a year later. Sadly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.080 --> 00:14:50.800
Anderson died in 2022 from complications&nbsp;
caused by cancer, at the age of 68.

00:14:50.800 --> 00:14:56.440
Actor Max Perlich played Ferris' snot-nosed&nbsp;
classmate who was "here" when Ben Stein called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.440 --> 00:15:01.320
out his name during roll call. Perlich may have&nbsp;
had little to say in the film, but his career is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.320 --> 00:15:05.560
an interesting one, starting with the fact that&nbsp;
he dropped out of high school to pursue acting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.560 --> 00:15:10.160
with his first movie role being in the John&nbsp;
Hughes film. Despite having very little to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.160 --> 00:15:13.800
Perlich was able to turn his part&nbsp;
into more movie and TV appearances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.800 --> 00:15:18.360
with his next projects including "Can't Buy&nbsp;
Me Love" and an episode of "21 Jump Street."

00:15:18.360 --> 00:15:22.240
Though he's rarely had what would be&nbsp;
considered meaty roles, Perlich managed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.240 --> 00:15:26.520
to carve a niche for himself in the industry,&nbsp;
and some of his better-known films include&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.520 --> 00:15:32.640
Mel Gibson's "Maverick" in 1994 and "Feeling&nbsp;
Minnesota" in 1996. Like many character actors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.640 --> 00:15:37.280
he has shown up on tons of TV shows over the&nbsp;
years, from "Nash Bridges" and "Gilmore Girls"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:37.280 --> 00:15:42.760
to "The Shield" and "Crossing Jordan." He appears&nbsp;
as Hank in "Twin Peaks: The Return" as well.

00:15:42.760 --> 00:15:47.360
But his biggest TV part was on the FX series&nbsp;
"Justified," where he played the sniveling mob&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.360 --> 00:15:52.480
boss' son Sammy Tonin in four episodes of&nbsp;
the critically acclaimed crime drama. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.480 --> 00:15:56.600
his constant work in the industry hasn't&nbsp;
been entirely thankless — Perlich won an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:56.600 --> 00:16:02.040
Independent Spirit Award for his supporting&nbsp;
performance in "Drugstore Cowboy" in 1990.

00:16:02.040 --> 00:16:04.960
Jeffrey Jones' screen acting&nbsp;
career took off in the mid '70s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.960 --> 00:16:08.960
thanks to being cast in projects like the&nbsp;
miniseries "The Adams Chronicles," and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.960 --> 00:16:13.840
by the 1980s he was an in-demand character&nbsp;
actor. After landing roles in "Easy Money,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.840 --> 00:16:18.600
"Amadeus," and other projects, he was cast&nbsp;
as Ed Rooney in "Ferris Bueller's Day Off."

00:16:18.600 --> 00:16:23.240
Jones continued to pick up major roles after his&nbsp;
"Bueller" success, starring in the short-lived,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.240 --> 00:16:27.840
Wes Craven-produced, off-the-wall forgotten&nbsp;
sitcom "The People Next Door." Surrounding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.840 --> 00:16:32.500
the failure of that show, he found safe harbor&nbsp;
in Tim Burton films like "Beetlejuice," "Ed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:32.500 --> 00:16:36.160
Wood," and "Sleepy Hollow." Other major&nbsp;
roles included being a conduit for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:36.160 --> 00:16:40.840
Dark Overlord in "Howard the Duck," "The Hunt&nbsp;
for Red October," "The Crucible," "The Devil's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:40.840 --> 00:16:46.920
Advocate," and a role as newspaper publisher A.W.&nbsp;
Merrick in the acclaimed HBO series "Deadwood."

00:16:46.920 --> 00:16:53.520
In 2003, Jones pleaded no contest to a charge of&nbsp;
employing a teenage boy to pose for nude photos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.520 --> 00:16:57.760
and in 2010 he was sentenced to community&nbsp;
service for failing to update his sex&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.760 --> 00:17:02.640
offender status. These legal issues led&nbsp;
to fewer screen appearances in the 2010s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.640 --> 00:17:07.520
but in 2019 he did reprise his role&nbsp;
of Merrick in "Deadwood: The Movie."

